Synthesis
  The Cayley-Dickson analogy is not that LRC literally is an algebra.  It is that every doubling row closes one old freedom while exposing a new defect layer.
  n=16 is the pure S-row test: 2-adic, clean, and close to the sedenion zero-divisor threshold.
  n=18 is the first attractive mixed row: CD row 2 (C) carrying odd core 9.  Its unprotected endpoints concentrate at layer 9, exactly the largest-proper-divisor payload.
  n=24 is the stress test: row 8 with odd core 3, lowest unit density in this window, and the loudest normalized pressure.
  My favorite remains n=18 because it is where the tower stops being pure doubling but has not yet become a large divisor-rich mess.
